{
  "dom_id": "step-q336174",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "The position $s(t)$ of a particle (in feet) at time $t$ minutes is given by \n\n$$\ns(t)=\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}},\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the speed of the particle when $t=1\\,\\text{min}.$\n\nThe speed of the particle is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{ft/min}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we calculate the velocity function by differentiating the position function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{t}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $t=1,$ the velocity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =-\\frac{4}{{1}^{3}} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed is $|-4|=4\\,\\text{ft/min}.$",
